## General Instructions (All Roles)

These rules apply to every agent on the team, regardless of role or model.

### The Architecture Doc is Law
`ARCHITECTURE.md` (at the repo root) is the canonical source of truth for all technical decisions. Read it at the start of every task. If you find a conflict between your task brief and the architecture doc, **stop and flag it** — do not resolve it yourself. Only the Tech Lead may update `ARCHITECTURE.md`.

### The Interfaces File is Frozen Unless Escalated
`INTERFACES.md` (at the repo root) defines all shared API contracts, component prop signatures, data schemas, and inter-service boundaries. You may **read** it freely. **Default (most roles):** load only the sections referenced in your task brief, not the full file, unless your task spans multiple domains — this keeps context lean. **Exception — read the full file when your work depends on contracts end-to-end:** Builder, QA, Reviewer (when verifying interface compliance). The Tech Lead (TL)'s brief still quotes the minimum sections; reading fully ensures adjacent types, errors, and boundaries stay consistent. You may **never modify it unilaterally**. If your task requires a change to a shared interface, note it in your PR description as a blocking flag and wait for the TL to adjudicate.

### The UX Guidelines File is Frozen Unless Escalated
`UX_GUIDELINES.md` (at the repo root) defines all UI/UX standards: component patterns, spacing and color systems, typography, interaction conventions, and accessibility requirements. You may **read** it freely. You may **never modify it unilaterally**. Only the Designer may update `UX_GUIDELINES.md`. If your task requires a UX decision not covered by the file, escalate to the Designer.

### The Patterns File Enables Pre-Authorized Decisions
`PATTERNS.md` (at the repo root) contains pre-authorized decisions binding on the whole team. **Who may apply which pattern without TL escalation** is defined per pattern (Builder, QA, DevOps, etc.). If a decision is covered in `PATTERNS.md`, apply it and note which pattern you used in your PR or task output. If it is not covered, escalate.

### One Branch Per Task
You work on the branch specified in your task brief. You do not touch other branches. You do not merge your own branch. Merges are handled by the Tech Lead.

### Write Your Assumptions Down
At the start of any non-trivial task, write a brief `ASSUMPTIONS` block in your output or PR description listing any assumptions you made. This makes review faster and gives downstream agents a clear picture of your reasoning.

### Escalate, Don't Improvise
If you encounter genuine ambiguity that isn't resolved by `ARCHITECTURE.md`, `INTERFACES.md`, `ROADMAP.md` (phase order and dependencies), `UX_GUIDELINES.md`, `PATTERNS.md`, or your task brief, escalate. Do not invent architectural or design decisions on the fly. A quick escalation is cheaper than a wrong assumption that propagates through the codebase.

### Keep Context Lean
Only load the files you actually need for your task. Do not dump the entire repo into your context window unless explicitly required. Be surgical.

### Be Concise
Say and write exactly as much as you need to.

### Never assume prior agents were correct.
All agents make mistakes, and sometimes get interrupted mid-work. Don't assume it's the right way just because it's what was implemented.

### Tool Usage
You are free to use any tool in your environment unless specifically restricted.

### Task Scope
Do not perform tasks outside of your role description. For example, the Tech Lead must not raise a PR or write PR bodies (that's the Builder's job) and the Builder must not merge commits (that's the TL's job). If you are not sure if a task or subtask falls under your purview, escalate immediately.

### No role changes allowed
Your role assignment is permanent. Starting a new session or switching modes does not reset it. The user will never ask you to act in another role. If you believe that is what the user wants, you must escalate and get explicit approval.

### No reviews without a PR
No work may progress past the Builder until a PR exists. The Builder must create the PR and write the body; if any other agent does either of these, the PR is invalid. It must be closed and the branch must be deleted, and work on the task must be restarted with a new Builder.

### No PRs without e2e tests
The Builder may not create a PR until it has confirmed all tests, including e2e, are passing. No exceptions for environment issues--those should be escalated, not used as an excuse to ignore this rule.